  "answer": "Both edits are complete and correct. Here's a summary of what I did.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe task was to fix route resolution in the request entry point. I edited only the two writable files that were broken.\n\n### `entry.mjs` (the entry point)\n**Before:** `dispatch` called `legacyResolve(path)` first, then fell back to the registry. This incorrectly made `/old/help` a production route and mixed the compatibility API into the production path.\n\n**After:** `dispatch(path)` delegates directly to `resolveFromRegistry(path, defaultRoutes)`. It no longer references `legacyResolve`, so the legacy API stays separate. Validation and matching policy are inherited from the resolver.\n\n### `registry.mjs` (the lower-level resolver)\n**Before:** It returned the first fallback it encountered (even if a later static/dynamic would match), and returned the first matching dynamic before ever checking statics — so the fixture's dynamic `/users/:id` would shadow the static `/users/new`.\n\n**After:** A single pass that:\n- Validates `path` (throws `TypeError` for non-string, empty, or non-`/`-prefixed input).\n- Returns immediately on the **first matching static** (static always wins over dynamic, regardless of declaration order).\n- Remembers the **first matching dynamic** but keeps scanning for a static.\n- Remembers the **first fallback** but only uses it if neither static nor dynamic matched.\n- Returns `null` when nothing matches and no fallback exists.\n\n### Correctness properties preserved\n- **Case-sensitive, trailing-slash-distinct** matching (delegated to `matchStatic`/`matchDynamic`, unchanged).\n- **Fresh `params` objects** on every call (static/fallback return new `{}`; dynamic builds a new object), so results are independent of later calls.\n- **Records are never mutated** — only read.\n- `legacy.mjs` and its `/old/help` behavior are untouched, as are the adapters and utilities.\n\n### Test trace (all pass)\n- `dispatch('/users/new')` → static wins → `{ route: 'user-new', params: {} }`\n- `dispatch('/users/Ada')` → `{ route: 'user-detail', params: { id: 'Ada' } }`\n- `dispatch('/missing')` → `{ route: 'not-found', params: {} }`\n- `routeHttp('/health?q=one').match` → `{ route: 'health', params: {} }`\n- `resolveFromRegistry('/x', [])` → `null`\n\nNote: I couldn't execute the test runner (no shell tool available), so I verified each case by tracing the logic against the fixture and test file.",
